def detect_field(msg):
    msg = msg.lower()
    if any(k in msg for k in ["sociology", "society", "culture", "community", "gender", "family", "social"]):
        return "Sociology"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["environment", "climate", "pollution", "waste", "flooding", "drainage", "sanitation", "ecology", "green"]):
        return "Environmental Science"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["geography", "gis", "map", "spatial", "landform", "river", "soil"]):
        return "Geography"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["land economy", "land value", "rent", "property", "valuation", "estate", "real estate", "land use", "compensation"]):
        return "Land Economy"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["architecture", "building design", "floor plan", "facade", "architect"]):
        return "Architecture"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["construction", "contractor", "building", "site management", "quantity", "cost", "cement", "engineer"]):
        return "Construction Management"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["settlement", "village", "town", "urban", "rural", "slum", "housing", "informal"]):
        return "Settlement & Development Planning"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["exam", "study", "test", "timetable", "assignment"]):
        return "Study & Exams"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["project", "thesis", "brief", "proposal"]):
        return "Project & Thesis"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["sample", "survey", "population", "questionnaire", "data", "bias"]):
        return "Research & Sampling"
    if any(k in msg for k in ["career", "job", "internship", "cv"]):
        return "Career"
    return "Development Planning"

def ai_brain(message, region, district, town):
    field = detect_field(message)
    pop = st.session_state.selected_pop
    user = st.session_state.username
    q = message.strip()
    
    # Common reasoning intro
    intro = f"Hello {user}, great question. Let's reason through this together for **{town}, {district}, {region} Region**.\n\n"
    intro += f"**Your Question:** \"{q}\"\n\n"
    intro += f"**Field Detected:** {field} | **Location Context:** {town} in {district} (Population: {pop:,}) | **Word Target:** Detailed brief (500-700 words)\n\n---\n\n"
    
    if field == "Study & Exams":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Your Situation\n\nYou are preparing for exams while also managing other work in {town}. The biggest mistake students make is studying everything equally. In {district}, where many students also work, travel, or deal with unstable light and data, you must study *smart*, not just long. The goal is not to finish the textbook, it is to master what the lecturer will actually test.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Why This Plan Works\n\nThink like your lecturer: They set questions from 3 places - lecture notes (60%), past questions (30%), and assignments (10%). If you spend 80% of your time on the textbook and 20% on past questions, you are doing it backwards. The reasoning is simple: Past questions show the pattern, notes show the detail, textbook shows the background. We will reverse that order.\n\n### 3. Detailed 7-Day Plan for {town}\n\n**Day 1-2 - Audit:** List all topics for each course. Mark each as Green (I know it), Yellow (I am unsure), Red (I don't know). In {town}, do this early morning 6-8am when mind is fresh. Spend 2 hours per course.\n\n**Day 3-4 - Attack Red Topics:** Focus only on Red topics. For each Red topic, watch one explanation, read notes, then solve 3 past questions on it. Do 50 minutes study, 10 minutes break. Repeat 3 times. Don't study in bed - sit at table, phone on silent.\n\n**Day 5 - Teach-Back:** Teach one topic to a friend in {district} or even to yourself out loud. If you cannot explain it in simple English like you are explaining to someone in {town} market, you don't know it yet. Go back and re-learn.\n\n**Day 6 - Full Mock:** Set timer, do a full past paper for 2 hours without checking answers. This trains your brain for pressure. Mark yourself honestly.\n\n**Day 7 - Light Review & Rest:** Only review your summary sheets and formulas. Sleep by 10pm. Your brain consolidates memory when you sleep, not when you cram all night.\n\n### 4. What To Do If You Have Only 2 Days Left\n\nIf time is short, do this: Day 1 - Only past questions + lecturer's slides. Day 2 - Only your mistakes + summary. Don't start new topics. It is better to know 70% very well than 100% poorly.\n\n### 5. Questions To Ask Yourself Tonight\n\n1. What is my weakest topic and why?\n2. Which past question appears every year?\n3. What will I do if light goes off during study?\n\nIf you follow this, you will walk into the exam in {district} with confidence, not panic. Start with the audit today - not tomorrow."
        return intro + body

    if field in ["Project & Thesis", "Development Planning", "Settlement & Development Planning"]:
        body = f"### 1. Understanding The Problem in {town}\n\nEvery good project in Development Planning starts with a real problem that real people in {town} feel every day. It could be traffic at Kejetia, flooding after rain, no clean water, poor drainage, overcrowded market, or unplanned buildings. Your job is not to solve all of Ghana - it is to solve one clear problem in one clear place in {district} very well. Lecturers fail projects that are too broad and vague.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - How To Think Like A Planner\n\nA planner thinks in 3 layers: **People, Place, and Policy.** First, People: Who lives in {town}? How many? What do they do? Young or old? Traders or farmers? Second, Place: Where is the problem? Near the market? Near the river? On a slope? Third, Policy: What does the {district} Assembly plan say? What does Ghana's National Development Plan say? If your proposal ignores any of these 3, it will fail in real life.\n\n### 3. Step-By-Step Method For {district}\n\n**Step 1 - Site Visit (2 Days):** Go to {town}. Take 30 photos - morning, afternoon, evening. Notice: Where does water go when it rains? Where do people walk? Where do cars park? Where is waste dumped? Talk to 10 people: 3 traders, 3 residents, 2 drivers, 2 Assembly staff. Ask: 'What is the biggest problem here and why has it not been fixed?'\n\n**Step 2 - Data (1 Day):** Population of {district} is {pop:,}. Use that for your sample calculation - don't guess. If you survey only {town}, you will have bias. Add 2 other towns in {district} to make your data fair. Use our Sample Calculator in this app to get the correct number.\n\n**Step 3 - Analysis (2 Days):** Draw 3 maps: Existing Land Use Map, Problem Map, Opportunity Map. Even hand-drawn maps are powerful if they are clear. Explain: Why is the problem here and not elsewhere? Is it because of slope, road, market location, or poor planning?\n\n**Step 4 - Proposal (2 Days):** Propose 3 options: Option A - Low Cost (e.g. clean drains, repaint, re-organise), Option B - Medium Cost (e.g. new drainage + walkway), Option C - High Cost (e.g. full redevelopment). Compare them by cost, time, and impact. Recommend the best one and justify why. Lecturers love this - it shows you reasoned, not just copied.\n\n**Step 5 - Report (1 Day):** Structure: Title, Abstract (150 words), Introduction, Problem Statement, Study Area ({town}, {district}), Methodology, Findings, Proposal, Conclusion, Recommendation, References. Keep language human and clear. Avoid big grammar that hides meaning.\n\n### 4. Example Applied To {town}\n\nSuppose the problem is flooding in {town}. Reasoning: Flooding happens because drains are blocked by waste + buildings are on waterways + rain is heavier now due to climate change. Solution is not just 'build bigger drain'. Solution is: Clear drains + educate traders not to dump + enforce no building on waterway + create green buffer. That is a planner's solution, not just an engineer's solution.\n\n### 5. What Your Supervisor Will Ask\n\n1. Why did you choose {town} and not another town?\n2. What is your data and is it biased?\n3. What are your 3 options and why did you pick one?\n4. How much will it cost and who will pay?\n5. What will {town} look like in 5 years if we do your plan?\n\nAnswer these in your report and you will get an A. Start with the site visit tomorrow morning."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Sociology":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding From Sociology Lens\n\nIn Sociology, we don't just ask 'What is happening in {town}?' We ask 'Why do people behave this way, and who benefits and who suffers?' For example, if traders in {town} sell on the road, a planner sees traffic. A sociologist asks: Why do they sell there? Is it because market stalls are too expensive? Because customers pass there? Because women need to be near home to care for children? That deeper 'why' is what makes your work human.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Key Sociological Theories To Use\n\nUse 3 simple theories: **1) Social Structure:** In {district}, society is organised by age, gender, tribe, income, and religion. A poor woman trader in {town} has different power from a rich shop owner. **2) Culture:** People in {town} have norms - e.g. respect for chiefs, market days, funeral customs. If your plan ignores culture, people will reject it. **3) Social Change:** {town} is changing - youth moving to Kumasi or Accra, phones changing trade, climate changing farming. Your plan must fit this change, not fight it.\n\n### 3. How To Study {town} Sociologically\n\n**Method:** Don't just count people. Do 5 in-depth interviews: Ask open questions like 'Tell me about your life in {town}', 'What has changed in 10 years?', 'Who decides what happens here?' Listen for 30 minutes each. Also do observation: Sit in the market for 1 hour and write what you see - who talks to who, who is excluded, who controls space.\n\n**Example:** If you study housing in {town}, don't just count houses. Ask: Who owns land? Can women own land? Do migrants feel welcome? How do families share rooms? That is sociology - it reveals power and inequality that numbers hide.\n\n### 4. Analysis & Application\n\nAfter your interviews, look for patterns: Do 4 out of 5 people say the same problem? Do women say something different from men? Do youth say something different from elders? That difference is your finding. In your report, use quotes: 'As one trader in {town} said...' That makes your work credible and human.\n\n### 5. What To Do Next\n\n1. Choose one sociological question: e.g. How does gender affect access to market space in {town}?\n2. Interview 5-8 people with different backgrounds.\n3. Write 1 page: What did you learn that you didn't know before?\n4. Link it to planning: If women are excluded, what should {district} Assembly do?\n\nSociology teaches you that planning is not about buildings - it is about people. If you understand people in {town}, your plan will work. If you don't, it will fail no matter how beautiful the drawing is."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Environmental Science":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding The Environmental Problem in {town}\n\nEnvironmental problems in {town}, {district} are not abstract - they are what you smell, see, and drink. Waste in drains, smoke from burning, flooding after rain, dirty water, loss of trees, heat in the afternoon. These are all connected. For example, when people cut trees in {town}, rain runs faster, drains block, flooding increases, mosquitoes increase. One problem creates another. That is why we must think in systems, not single problems.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Cause, Effect, and Solution Chain\n\nLet's reason: **Cause:** Population of {district} is {pop:,} and growing, but waste collection has not grown. **Effect:** Waste goes into drains and river. Drains block, water has nowhere to go, floods houses. Flood water mixes with toilet waste, people get sick. **Root Cause:** Not just 'people are dirty' - it is system failure: No bins, no regular collection, no recycling, no enforcement. If you only blame people, your solution will fail. If you fix the system, people will change.\n\n### 3. Detailed Assessment For {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Observe:** Walk around {town} for 1 hour. Note: Where is waste? Where is water dirty? Where is air smoky? Take photos with location.\n\n**Step 2 - Measure Simply:** You don't need expensive lab. Count: How many bins in {town}? How many drains are blocked? How many trees remain? How many hours does water flow per day? These simple numbers are powerful.\n\n**Step 3 - Propose Solutions in 3 Levels:** **Level 1 - Household:** Separate waste, compost organic, don't burn plastic. **Level 2 - Community:** Community clean-up every Saturday, community bins, community tree planting. **Level 3 - Assembly:** {district} Assembly should provide regular collection, enforce by-laws, create green buffer along river, and plant trees on school compounds.\n\n### 4. Example - Flooding Solution\n\nIf flooding is the issue in {town}, don't just propose 'big drain'. Propose: 1) Clear existing drains, 2) Ban building on waterways, 3) Plant grass and trees on slopes to slow water, 4) Create rainwater harvesting in homes, 5) Early warning via community WhatsApp when rain is coming. This is a complete environmental solution - it is cheaper and more sustainable than concrete alone.\n\n### 5. What To Do This Week\n\n1. Visit {town} and list 5 environmental problems you see.\n2. Pick the most urgent one and explain its cause-effect chain in 1 page.\n3. Talk to 3 elders: 'How was {town} environment 20 years ago vs now?'\n4. Write your proposal with cost - e.g. 100 trees cost how much? Who will water them?\n\nEnvironmental science is not about complaining - it is about proving the link between environment and health in {town}, and proposing a solution that people can actually do."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Geography":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Geography of {town}\n\nGeography asks: Why is {town} where it is? Why does it look like this? The answer is in location, landform, water, soil, climate, and human decisions. {town} in {district} did not grow by accident - it grew near a road, river, market, or fertile land. Understanding that helps you explain everything else - why traffic is here, why flooding is there, why rich houses are on the hill and poor houses are in the valley.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Spatial Thinking\n\nGeographers think spatially: **Pattern:** Where are things? **Process:** Why are they there? **Interaction:** How do they affect each other? For example, in {town}, pattern: Market is in the centre, houses spread out. Process: Market grew because roads meet there. Interaction: Market causes traffic, traffic causes pollution, pollution affects health. If you can explain this chain, you have done geography.\n\n### 3. How To Do A Geographical Study of {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Map It:** Draw a sketch map of {town} - roads, river, market, schools, houses. Mark north. Use Google Maps or QGIS if you can. Even a hand map is powerful.\n\n**Step 2 - Describe Physical Geography:** What is the relief? Flat or hilly? What is soil type? Where does water flow? How much rain? How hot? This explains why farming or building is difficult in some parts of {district}.\n\n**Step 3 - Describe Human Geography:** Population of {district} is {pop:,}. Where do people live? Dense in centre, sparse outside? What jobs? Trading, farming, teaching? How do people move? Tro-tro, motorbike, walking? Where do they get water and food?\n\n**Step 4 - Explain Change:** How has {town} changed in 10 years? Has it grown towards Kumasi or Accra road? Has farmland become houses? Why? This is the most interesting part - it shows you understand process, not just description.\n\n### 4. Example Analysis\n\nIf {town} is growing fast, reasoning: Growth is because of road improvement + new school + cheaper land than Kumasi. Effect: Farmland is lost, traffic increases, water pressure drops. Solution: {district} Assembly should plan expansion - reserve land for school, market, green space before it is all built. That is geography applied to planning.\n\n### 5. Next Steps\n\n1. Draw map of {town} today.\n2. Add 5 labels: Market, River, Main Road, School, Problem Area.\n3. Write 1 paragraph: Why is {town} growing this way?\n4. Propose 1 spatial solution: e.g. New road, new market location, green buffer.\n\nGood geography is not memorising capitals - it is explaining why {town} is the way it is, and what it will become if we do nothing."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Land Economy":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Land Economy in {town}\n\nLand Economy is about value: Why is land in {town} near the market worth 10x more than land on the outskirts of {district}? Why does rent go up when a new road is built? Why do some families become rich because they owned land early, while others become poor because they sold too cheap? If you understand land value, you understand power in {town}.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - What Determines Land Value\n\nLand value in {town} depends on 5 factors: **1) Location:** Near road, market, school = high value. **2) Accessibility:** Can tro-tro reach it? **3) Infrastructure:** Water, light, drain? **4) Legal:** Does it have title? Is it family land or stool land? **5) Future Expectation:** Will government build something nearby? For example, if {district} Assembly announces a new market in {town}, land around it will jump in price overnight - even before building starts. That is speculation.\n\n### 3. Practical Analysis For {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Compare:** Find 3 plots in {town}: One near market, one middle, one outskirts. Ask: How much is rent? How much is sale price? You will see a clear pattern - value drops with distance.\n\n**Step 2 - Explain Tenure:** In {district}, most land is stool/family land, not state land. That means to buy land, you negotiate with family/chief, not just government. Problems: Double sale, no documents, boundary disputes. This is why many people in {town} build without permit - the system is slow and confusing.\n\n**Step 3 - Propose Fair Solution:** Suggest: {district} Assembly should work with chiefs to document land, provide simple titles, and use land value capture - e.g. when government builds road, part of the increase in land value should fund the road, not just go to speculators. Also suggest: Affordable land for youth and women, not just rich buyers.\n\n### 4. Example - Valuation\n\nIf a plot in {town} centre is 50x100ft and sells for GHS 80,000, while same size on outskirts sells for GHS 15,000, the difference is location + infrastructure, not soil. A valuer would explain this with comparable evidence, not guess. Always use 3 comparables.\n\n### 5. What To Do Next\n\n1. Interview 3 landowners in {town}: How did you get land? How much? Any dispute?\n2. Visit {district} Lands Commission: What is process for title?\n3. Write 1 page: Why is land expensive in {town} and who benefits?\n4. Propose 1 policy: e.g. Land bank for affordable housing.\n\nLand economy is not just about money - it is about justice. Who owns {town}? Who benefits when {town} grows? Answer that and your work will stand out."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Architecture":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Architecture in {town}\n\nArchitecture in {town} is not just about beautiful buildings - it is about how people live in heat, rain, and culture. A good building in {district} must be cool without AC, dry in rain, and respectful of how families live - e.g. compound house where grandmother, parents, and children share courtyard, kitchen outside, toilet separate. If you design a European glass house in {town}, it will be hot, expensive, and people will hate it.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Climate + Culture + Cost\n\nGood architecture reasons in 3 ways: **Climate:** In {town}, sun is strong, rain is heavy. So you need: Big roof overhang to shade walls, cross-ventilation (windows opposite each other), high ceiling to let hot air rise, and raised floor to avoid flooding. **Culture:** People in {town} value: Courtyard for gathering, veranda for sitting, kitchen separate for smoke, and privacy for women. **Cost:** Most families cannot afford imported materials. Use local: Laterite, compressed earth bricks, local timber, and good design to keep cost low.\n\n### 3. Design Process For A Building in {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Brief:** Who will use it? Family of 6? School? Clinic? What rooms? What budget? What site in {district}?\n\n**Step 2 - Site Analysis:** Visit site in {town}. Where is sun morning vs afternoon? Where does water flow? Where is road? Where is neighbour's window? Don't block their air.\n\n**Step 3 - Concept:** Sketch 3 options: Option A - Compound style (rooms around courtyard), Option B - Linear (rooms in line along veranda), Option C - Modern (open plan). Compare by ventilation, cost, and cultural fit. Choose best and explain why.\n\n**Step 4 - Detailed Design:** Floor plan, sections, elevations. Show: Roof, windows, doors, materials. Add: Rainwater tank, solar for light, and green space. A building that harvests rain in {town} is smarter than one that depends only on pipe water.\n\n### 4. Example - House For {town}\n\nA 3-bedroom house in {town} should have: Veranda facing breeze, living room with 2 opposite windows, kitchen with outside door, and toilet with ventilation. Roof: Long-span aluminium with overhang 900mm. Walls: Sandcrete or compressed earth. Floor: Raised 600mm to avoid flood. This house will be cooler, cheaper to maintain, and people will love it.\n\n### 5. Next Steps\n\n1. Sketch your site in {town} and mark sun and wind.\n2. Draw 2 floor plan options and compare.\n3. Ask 3 residents: What do you like/dislike about your house?\n4. Write 1 paragraph: How does your design respect climate and culture of {district}?\n\nArchitecture is not about showing off - it is about making life in {town} more comfortable, affordable, and beautiful."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Construction Management":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Construction in {town}\n\nConstruction Management in {town}, {district} is about delivering a building on time, on budget, and safely - even when cement price goes up, rain stops work, or workers don't come. Many projects in Ghana fail not because of design, but because of poor management: No programme, no budget control, no safety, no communication. Your job as manager is to prevent chaos.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Time, Cost, Quality Triangle\n\nYou cannot have all 3 perfect at once. If you want it fast + cheap in {town}, quality will suffer. If you want it fast + good quality, cost will be high. If you want it cheap + good quality, it will be slow. A good manager in {district} explains this to the client early and agrees on priority. For example, for a school in {town}, quality and time matter more than cheap - children must be safe and school must open in September.\n\n### 3. Detailed Management Plan For A Project in {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Programme:** Break work into: Site clearing (1 week), Foundation (2 weeks), Blockwork (3 weeks), Roof (2 weeks), Finishing (4 weeks). Make a Gantt chart. Add buffer for rain in {district} - don't plan concrete work in peak rain without cover.\n\n**Step 2 - Budget:** List: Cement, sand, blocks, steel, timber, labour, transport from Kumasi/Accra to {town}, equipment, contingency (10%). In {town}, transport cost is high - factor it. If cement is GHS 100 in Kumasi, it may be GHS 115 in {town} due to transport. Don't ignore this.\n\n**Step 3 - Procurement:** Order materials early. Store cement dry, steel covered. Check deliveries - count bags, don't trust driver alone.\n\n**Step 4 - Quality & Safety:** Check foundation depth, block strength, and concrete mix. Provide helmets, boots, and first aid. One accident in {town} can stop site for weeks.\n\n**Step 5 - Communication:** Daily: 10-min site meeting. Weekly: Report to client with photos and % complete. If there is delay, tell client early with solution, not late with excuse.\n\n### 4. Example Problem & Solution\n\nProblem: Rain delays foundation in {town} by 1 week. Bad manager: Does nothing, blames rain. Good manager: Uses delay to order roof materials, re-schedules labour to indoor work, and informs client that completion will shift by 3 days, not 7, because of re-planning. That is management.\n\n### 5. What To Do Next\n\n1. Pick a building in {town} - e.g. a classroom or shop.\n2. Write its programme in 6 steps with time.\n3. Write its budget with 8 items + contingency.\n4. List 3 risks in {district} (rain, price increase, labour) and how you will manage each.\n\nConstruction management is not about shouting on site - it is about planning, controlling, and communicating so that the building in {town} is finished well and people trust you for the next job."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Research & Sampling":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Your Research in {district}\n\nResearch is not just collecting questionnaires - it is answering one clear question with honest data from {town} and {district}. The biggest error in Ghanaian student research is bias: Surveying only your friends, only people near KNUST, only people in {town} centre, and then claiming it represents all of {district}. That is data bias, and it is the core problem of this app.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Why Sampling Matters\n\nPopulation of {district} is {pop:,}. You cannot interview everyone, so you sample. But if your sample is not representative, your conclusion is wrong. For example, if you survey only traders in {town} market about flooding, you will miss farmers on the outskirts who suffer more. If you survey only in English, you will miss older people who speak Twi. Reasoning: Good sampling = Fair representation.\n\n### 3. Detailed Sampling Plan For {town}\n\n**Step 1 - Define Population:** Who are you studying? All residents of {district}? Or only traders in {town}? Be precise.\n\n**Step 2 - Calculate Sample:** For 95% confidence, 5% margin, you need 300-400 people. Use our Sample Calculator in this app - it already uses {pop:,} for {district}. Don't use 50 people and claim it represents 400,000.\n\n**Step 3 - Stratify:** Divide {district} into 3 strata: Urban ({town} centre), Peri-urban (edges), Rural (villages). Take sample from each. E.g. 40% urban, 30% peri-urban, 30% rural. This avoids bias.\n\n**Step 4 - Randomise:** Don't pick only people you know. Use systematic: Every 5th house on a street in {town}, or every 3rd trader in market. This is fair.\n\n**Step 5 - Questionnaire:** Keep it short - 15 questions max. Use simple English + Twi translation. Start with easy questions, end with sensitive ones (income). Test with 5 people first, fix confusing questions.\n\n### 4. Data Bias Checker\n\nAsk yourself: 1) Did I include women and men equally? 2) Did I include youth and elders? 3) Did I include rich and poor areas in {district}? 4) Did I include different towns, not just {town}? If you answer No to any, your data is biased. Fix it before you analyse.\n\n### 5. Next Steps\n\n1. Write your research question in 1 sentence.\n2. Calculate sample using {pop:,} in this app.\n3. List 3 towns in {district} you will survey (not just {town}).\n4. Draft 10 questions and test with a friend in {town}.\n\nGood research in {district} is not about big grammar - it is about honest, fair data that truly represents the people of {town}."
        return intro + body

    if field == "Career":
        body = f"### 1. Understanding Career in {region}\n\nCareer is not just 'get a job' - it is building skills that make you valuable in {district} and Ghana. Many graduates in {town} wait for government job, but the real opportunities are in: District Assemblies, NGOs, Real Estate, GIS, Construction, and Consulting. The question is: What can you do that others cannot?\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Skills That Pay\n\nIn {region}, 3 skills pay: **1) GIS & Mapping:** Every Assembly needs maps. If you can do QGIS and produce a land use map for {town}, you will get work. **2) Proposal Writing:** NGOs need people who can write clear proposals for {district}. **3) Data & Excel:** If you can analyse survey data for {town} and present it clearly, you are ahead of 80% of graduates.\n\n### 3. Career Paths From {town}\n\n**Path A - District Planner:** Work at {district} Assembly. Salary stable, impact direct. Need: Degree + National Service + GIS + Planning knowledge.\n\n**Path B - GIS Analyst / Consultant:** Work for private firm or NGO. Higher pay, project-based. Need: QGIS, AutoCAD, Google Earth, and portfolio of 3 projects for {town}.\n\n**Path C - Real Estate / Land Economy:** Work in valuation, property management. Need: Land knowledge + negotiation + Excel.\n\n**Path D - Research & Academia:** Masters + PhD, teach at KNUST. Need: Good grades + publications + passion for teaching.\n\n### 4. 90-Day Action Plan\n\n**Days 1-30:** Learn QGIS (YouTube, 1 hour daily). Produce 1 map of {town} - Land Use Map.\n\n**Days 31-60:** Learn AutoCAD basics. Produce 1 site plan for {town} market or school.\n\n**Days 61-90:** Build portfolio: 3 projects, 1 CV, 1 LinkedIn. Apply to 10 places: {district} Assembly, 3 NGOs, 3 firms, 2 schools. Don't wait for perfect - apply now.\n\n### 5. What To Do Today\n\n1. Write down your top 2 career interests.\n2. Find 1 person in {district} doing that job and ask them how they got it.\n3. Start 1 skill today - QGIS or Excel - 1 hour.\n\nYour location in {town} is not a disadvantage - it is your advantage. You understand {district} better than someone from Accra. Use that local knowledge to get the job."
        return intro + body

    # Default - Development Planning general
    body = f"### 1. Understanding The Issue in {town}\n\nLet's think deeply about this for {town}, {district}. Development is not just building roads or houses - it is improving how people live, move, work, and feel safe. In {town}, development must fit the people, the land, and the culture. If we copy Accra's solution to {town} without thinking, it will fail. We must reason from local reality.\n\n### 2. Reasoning - Step By Step\n\n**Step 1 - What is the real problem?** Don't accept the first answer. If people say 'traffic', ask why - Is it because market is on road? Because no parking? Because tro-tro stops anywhere? The real problem is often deeper than the symptom.\n\n**Step 2 - Who is affected?** In {district}, who suffers most? Traders? School children? Elderly? Women carrying load? A good plan prioritises the most vulnerable, not just car owners.\n\n**Step 3 - What are the options?** Always propose 3 options: Low cost (organise better), Medium cost (small infrastructure), High cost (full redevelopment). Compare by cost, time, impact, and sustainability. Choose the best and justify it - that is reasoning.\n\n**Step 4 - What will happen if we do nothing?** In 5 years, {town} population will grow, problem will be worse. Use population {pop:,} to show urgency. This convinces Assembly to act.\n\n### 3. Practical Plan For {town}\n\n**Week 1 - Understand:** Visit {town}, take photos, talk to 10 people, collect data.\n\n**Week 2 - Analyse:** Draw maps, list problems, rank them by urgency and impact.\n\n**Week 3 - Propose:** Draft proposal with 3 options, choose best, estimate cost.\n\n**Week 4 - Present:** Write report with clear language, maps, and recommendation. Practice presenting in 5 minutes: Problem (1 min), Data (1 min), Options (2 mins), Recommendation (1 min).\n\n### 4. Example - If Problem Is Market Congestion in {town}\n\nReasoning: Market is congested because stalls are too small + traders spill to road + no loading bay + waste blocks drain. Solution is not just 'build bigger market'. Solution is: Re-organise stalls, create loading bay, provide bins and daily collection, and create pedestrian walkway. That is a complete, human solution.\n\n### 5. Final Advice\n\n1. Always start with people in {town}, not with buildings.\n2. Always use data from {district}, not just guess.\n3. Always propose a solution that {district} Assembly can afford and maintain.\n4. Always ask: Will this still work in 5 years?\n\nIf you follow this reasoning, your work in {town} will be professional, human, and practical - not just theory. Start with Step 1 tomorrow morning."
    return intro + body

